{
	title: 'Remote terminals show the host theme',
	spec: "A remote terminal (./run -r, task tr) shows the host's colour theme, as the web does; /theme typed there changes it at once. Invariant: a remote terminal's colours follow the host's active theme, never only the remote machine's own plugins/color-theme.ts. Event { type: theme, name } (common/protocol.ts, event-check.ts): the host sends it to a client on connect unless the active theme is hal (theme.greet in host/commands/theme.ts; a user's own color-theme.ts file is not a theme name and is never sent), and to every client after /theme switches. host/host-theme.ts follow(name), used only by main.remote: hal is the built-in look the remote terminal starts in (follow('hal') before connecting); it removes this machine's color-theme.ts plugin and the previously followed theme, then loads themes/<name>.ts from this checkout through the plugin loader, whose onChange repaints. The name must match [A-Za-z0-9_-]+, so a host can name only a tracked theme file; an invalid name or a theme missing from this checkout leaves the look unchanged and shows a notice. Local terminals ignore the event: they watch the host's plugins/ directly.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Known gaps: the local plugins/ watcher reloads color-theme.ts if it changes while a remote terminal runs; a switch to hal while the remote is disconnected is missed until the next /theme or restart, because connect stays silent for hal (sending it always broke tests counting connect events).'
	],
	needs: ['d3', 'tr'],
	foldInto: ['d3']
}
